-- title:
--   Some element of J is a unit at Igusa-type valuations
-- statement:
--   Throughout, fix a prime $q \ge 5$, a nonzero natural number $M'$ with $q \nmid M'$, and a prime $\ell \ge 3$ with $\ell \neq q$ and $\ell \nmid M'$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic $0$ and $\xi \in L$ a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity; the hypothesis `hι` asks for a ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\xi) = \exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ which, by `hK`, is the $L$-subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ generated by the coefficientwise image of [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionField ((qℓ)^2 M') (levelH (qℓ) M')`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L79), the field of $q$-expansions over $\mathbb{Q}$ attached to the congruence subgroup $\Gamma_H$ of level $(q\ell)^2M'$ with $H =$ `levelH (qℓ) M'` the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/(q\ell)^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q\ell)^\times$, i.e. the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q\ell$.
--
--   Let $A$ be a discrete valuation ring which is a domain with fraction field $L$, henselian, with algebraically closed residue field, such that $q$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ (`hAq`) and $\xi$ lies in the image of $A$ (`hξA`); $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. Let $j \in K$ be nonzero, with Laurent expansion the coefficientwise image of the rational $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of the modular invariant (`hj`). Write $C :=$ `chartAlgFin A ↥K j` for the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ consisting of the elements integral over $A[j]$, and `jChartFin` for $j$ regarded as an element of $C$. Let $\varpi$ generate the maximal ideal of $A$ (`hϖ`) and let $\varpi_t \in A$ satisfy $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = qu$ for some unit $u$ (`hϖt`). Let $y$ be a maximal ideal of $C$ containing $\varpi$ (`hy`, `hϖy`), supersingular in the sense of `hss`: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the element $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), the set of those $j_0 \in \Omega$ such that every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $j_0$ has no nonzero $q$-torsion point.
--
--   Let $X :=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) be the scheme obtained by glueing $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the chart algebra at $j$ and $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the chart algebra at $j^{-1}$ along the middle chart, with structural morphism `toBase` to $\mathrm{Spec}\,A$ and canonical morphism `ιFin` from `XFin`$= \mathrm{Spec}\,C$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hArig` (a Drinfeld-chart hypothesis, with seven clauses summarised below) asserts: for every point $z$ of $X$, every stalk element $\varpi_z$ equal to the germ at $z$ of the function on $X$ obtained from $\varpi$ through `toBase` and lying in the maximal ideal of the stalk, every point $y'$ of $\mathrm{Spec}\,C$ with `ιFin` $(y') = z$ subject to the supersingularity condition `hss'` (the analogue of `hss` for $y'$), there exist a complete discrete valuation ring $W$, a ring homomorphism $\sigma : A \to W$ with maximal ideal generated by $\sigma(\varpi)$, power series $f,u,v \in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u,v$ units and $f \equiv X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and a ring isomorphism $e$ from the adic completion of the stalk $\mathcal{O}_{X,z}$ onto $S := W[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))v - fu)$ such that, writing `toC` for the completion map, `mkS` for the quotient map onto $S$ and `germY` for the composite $C \to \mathcal{O}_{X,z}$ through the germ at $z$ on the image of `ιFin`: (i) $e$ carries the germ of each $a \in A$ to the class of the constant $C(\sigma a)$; (ii) for every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and every $\tau : K \simeq_L K$ with `IsLevelAutAt L (qℓ) ξ (qℓ) ((qℓ)^2 M') (levelH (qℓ) M') γ⁻¹ K τ` — that is, $\tau$ acts on quotients of integral $q$-expansions of modular forms of level $\Gamma_H$ as the slash action by `conjElemN (qℓ) γ⁻¹`, read through any $\iota$ as above — the subalgebra $C$ is $\tau$-stable; (iii) for such $\tau$ with moreover $\gamma \in \Gamma(\ell)$, the restriction of $\tau$ to $C$ is the identity modulo $y'$; (iv) for such $\tau$ acting trivially modulo $y'$, there are a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, an element $c \in W$ and a matrix $M \in \mathrm{M}_2(W)$ with: $\theta$ intertwining $e \circ$ `toC` $\circ$ `germY` with the action of $\tau$ on $C$; $\theta$ fixing all constants; $\theta(X_j) \equiv \sum_i M_{ij}X_i$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by the classes of $X_0,X_1$; $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$, $M_{ij} \equiv c\,\gamma_{ij}$ modulo the maximal ideal of $W$; $c \equiv 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(\ell)$; and $c \not\equiv 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity; (v) a separation clause: for integers $a_1,b_1,a_2,b_2$ and primes $P_1,P_2$ of $S$, each not containing both classes of $X_0,X_1$, each containing the class of $C(\sigma\varpi)$ and each containing some element $C(a_i)X_0 + C(b_i)X_1 + h$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, if $q \nmid a_1b_2 - a_2b_1$ then the contractions of $P_1$ and $P_2$ to the stalk along $e \circ$ `toC` differ; (vi) for a prime $P$ of $S$ not containing both classes of $X_0,X_1$, containing the class of $C(\sigma\varpi)$ and containing an element $C(1)X_0 + C(0)X_1 + h$ with $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, an element $a \in C$ has `toC` $($`germY` $a)$ in the contraction of $P$ along $e$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of the maximal ideal of $A$; (vii) the element [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L (qℓ)`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) (the Laurent series of $j$ with all exponents multiplied by $q\ell$) lies in $K$ and in $C$, and there are $a_0 \in A$ congruent to it modulo $y'$, an integer $e_0 \ge 1$ and $h \in (X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ such that $\sum_{i=0}^{e_0} \mathrm{coeff}_{(i,e_0-i)}(h)\,a^ib^{e_0-i}$ is a unit of $W$ for all $a,b \in W$ not both in the maximal ideal with $a^qb - ab^q$ in the maximal ideal, and such that $e$ sends the germ of the difference of `jqNModC` and $a_0$ to the class of $h$.
--
--   The statement then fixes one such configuration: a point $z$, the germ $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$ lying in the maximal ideal of the stalk, a point $y'$ of $\mathrm{Spec}\,C$ over $z$ with the supersingularity property `hss'` and with $y'$ equal to $y$ as an ideal (`hy'y`); and one witness $W_1, \sigma_1, f_1, u_1, v_1, e_1$ with $W_1$ a complete discrete valuation ring, $\sigma_1 : A \to W_1$ sending $\varpi$ to a generator of the maximal ideal, $u_1,v_1$ units, $f_1$ congruent to $X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and $e_1$ an isomorphism from the completed stalk onto $S = W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))v_1 - f_1u_1)$; the hypothesis `hW₁` asserts for this data exactly the seven clauses (i)–(vii) listed above.
--
--   Next, `hJ` defines the ideal $J$ of $C$ as the infimum of all ideals of the form $(\tau|_C)^{-1}$ of the preimage under $e_1 \circ$ `toC` $\circ$ `germY` of the ideal of $S$ generated by the classes of $C(\sigma_1\varpi_t)$, $X_0$ and $X_1$, taken over all $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and all level automorphisms $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$. The hypothesis `hB` defines $B$ as the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ generated over $C$ by all $x \in K$ with $x\varpi_t \in J$, that is, the affine blow-up chart $C[J/\varpi_t]$.
--
--   Let $W$ be a valuation subring of $K$ containing $B$ (`hBW`). Three hypotheses describe $B$ and $W$: `hR1` states that $C \subseteq B$ and that every element of $K$ is a quotient of two elements of $B$ with nonzero denominator; `hR2` states that $A \to B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation and that $B/\varpi B$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; `hR3` (five clauses) states that the elements of $L$ lying in $W$ are exactly those in the image of $A$, that the maximal ideal of $W$ is generated by $\varpi$, that $W$ is a discrete valuation ring, that an element of $C$ lies in $y$ exactly when it lies in the maximal ideal of $W$, and that $W$ is the localisation of $B$ at that centre (an $f \in K$ lies in $W$ precisely when $fh = g$ for some $g,h \in B$ with $h$ outside the maximal ideal of $W$).
--
--   The hypothesis `hEQ` (five clauses) records the Drinfeld description of the special fibre: (a) for every $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-algebra structure on the residue field $\kappa$ of $A$ there is a surjective ring homomorphism $\rho$ from $B$ onto [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q κ`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) $= \kappa[X_0,X_1]/($`drinfeldPoly` $- 1)$ whose kernel consists of the elements of the maximal ideal of $W$, which is compatible with $A \to \kappa$, and which is equivariant in the following sense: for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and a level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $W$, there is $c \in \mathbb{F}_{q^2}^\times$ with $($`redQ q γ`$, c)$ in [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) (the kernel of $(g,c) \mapsto \det(g)\,c^{q+1}$) such that $\rho \circ \tau =$ `hAction` $(\,$`redQ q γ`$, c) \circ \rho$ on $B$, and $c \neq 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity; (b) $B$ is stable under every level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$; (c) every prime $Q$ of $B$ containing $\varpi$ is reached by the group action: there are $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and a level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ such that every $b \in B$ lying in the maximal ideal of $W$ has $\tau b \in B$ with class in $Q$; (d) if $b \in B$ has $\tau b$ in the maximal ideal of $W$ for all $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and all level automorphisms $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ (whenever $\tau b \in B$), then $\varpi$ divides $b$ in $B$; (e) a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ which preserves $y$ on $C$ also preserves $W$.
--
--   Finally, let $O$ be a subring of $K$. The hypothesis `hO` asks for a nonzero $a \in J$ and, with $B_a$ the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ generated over $C$ by all $x$ with $xa \in J$, a maximal ideal $P$ of $B_a$ such that $O$ is the localisation of $B_a$ at $P$ (an $f \in K$ lies in $O$ precisely when $fh = g$ with $g,h \in B_a$, $h \notin P$), every element of $y$ is a non-unit of $O$, and $B$ is not contained in $O$. Further, $O \subseteq W$ (`hOW`), $O$ is local (`hOloc`) and Noetherian (`hOnoe`), and $C \subseteq O$ (`hCO`). The hypothesis `hIgStab` states that every valuation subring $V$ of $K$ whose intersection with $L$ is exactly the image of $A$, which contains $\varpi$ in its maximal ideal, which contains $C$, and for which some $c \in C$ is residually transcendental in the sense that $p(c)$ lies in $V$ but not in its maximal ideal for every monic $p \in A[T]$, is stable under all level automorphisms $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$: $f \in V$ if and only if $\tau f \in V$.
--
--   The conclusion concerns an arbitrary valuation subring $V$ of $K$ subject to two conditions: the elements of $L$ lying in $V$ are exactly those in the image of $A$; and if $\varpi$ lies in $V$ then it lies in the maximal ideal of $V$. For such a $V$, assuming in addition that $j \in V$ and that $j$ is residually transcendental at $V$, in the sense that for every monic polynomial $p$ over $A$ the element $p(j)$, whenever it lies in $V$, does not lie in the maximal ideal of $V$, there exists an element $c$ of $C$ such that $c \in J$ and such that $c$, whenever it lies in $V$, does not lie in the maximal ideal of $V$. Thus no Igusa-type valuation ring of $K$ has $J$ inside its centre on $C$.
--
--   This is the cosupport statement for the weighted centre $J$ used in the analysis of the blown-up supersingular chart of a modular curve of full level $q\ell$ over a henselian base: it says that the Igusa-type valuation rings of $K$ (constants exactly $A$, $\varpi$ in the maximal ideal, $j$ residually transcendental) all fail to contain $J$ in their centre. It is invoked by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevel.exists_verticalUnit_chartAlgFin_mem_maximalIdeal_iff_of_end_blowupChart`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevel.exists_verticalUnit_chartAlgFin_mem_maximalIdeal_iff_of_end_blowupChart), where it supplies the element of $J$ that is a unit along the Igusa components of the special fibre.
-- source:
